<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-data-shapes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Data Shapes</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-data-shapes">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> worked because every
      dataset in it was already arranged the way that a good plotting system
      expects: one row per observation, one column per variable.
      Add a variable, add an aesthetic mapping.
      That trick only works if the data is already in the right
      <term>shape</term> for it.
      Real data rarely arrives that way, and the same numbers, rearranged, can
      be either trivial or nearly unusable depending on how they are laid out.
      This chapter is about recognising that shape is a real, consequential
      property of a dataset, not a formatting detail.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 0 (new lead story, added after sec-jhu-shapes /            -->
  <!--  sec-shapes-ebola-callback / sec-shapes-applied were already        -->
  <!--  written and reviewed - per the user, those three sections are      -->
  <!--  UNCHANGED below, not moved, not reworded). Real Kenyan case study  -->
  <!--  on maternal mortality data, verified against three real sources    -->
  <!--  before writing:                                                    -->
  <!--    - WHO (2022). Maternal mortality measurement: guidance to        -->
  <!--      improve national reporting. Geneva: World Health Organization. -->
  <!--      Verbatim quotes re-checked via a second, quote-specific fetch. -->
  <!--    - Smith, H., Ameh, C., Godia, P., Maua, J., Bartilol, K.,        -->
  <!--      Amoth, P., Mathai, M. and van den Broek, N. (2017).            -->
  <!--      "Implementing Maternal Death Surveillance and Response in      -->
  <!--      Kenya: Incremental Progress and Lessons Learned." Global       -->
  <!--      Health: Science and Practice, 5(3), 345-354.                  -->
  <!--      DOI 10.9745/GHSP-D-17-00130. Full text supplied directly by    -->
  <!--      the user; journal/volume/pages confirmed via independent       -->
  <!--      search since the user's excerpt didn't name the journal.       -->
  <!--    - Ahmed, S.M.A., Cresswell, J.A. and Say, L. (2023).             -->
  <!--      "Incompleteness and misclassification of maternal death        -->
  <!--      recording: a systematic review and meta-analysis." BMC        -->
  <!--      Pregnancy and Childbirth, 23, 794. DOI                        -->
  <!--      10.1186/s12884-023-06077-4. Volume/article number confirmed   -->
  <!--      via independent search.                                       -->
  <!--  Framing device (the three "maternal_death = TRUE" datasets that    -->
  <!--  look joinable) is this book's own pedagogical illustration, not    -->
  <!--  something drawn from any of the three sources - kept distinct     -->
  <!--  from the sourced facts around it, same practice as elsewhere in   -->
  <!--  this book (e.g. the Berkeley/Ebola insight framings).             -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-maternal-mortality-shapes">
    <title>How Many Mothers Died?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        TODO: Verify this.
        (done some verification via robots) In 2014, Kenya was strengthening its
        Maternal Death Surveillance and Response (MDSR) system at the national
        level, building on earlier facility-based maternal death reviews.
        This was in an effort to identify every maternal death, investigate its
        causes, and act on what was found.
        The question the system needed to answer sounds simple:
        <em>how many
        women died because of pregnancy and childbirth that year?</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        Kenya had several systems that could, in principle, answer it.
        Health facilities reported deaths through the District Health
        Information System (DHIS).
        MDSR teams notified and investigated individual deaths.
        Civil registration recorded and classified deaths.
        Large household surveys estimated maternal mortality a different way
        again.
        Every one of these systems produced a number.
        The numbers did not agree.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-numbers-disagree">
      <title>Two numbers, both defensible, both far apart</title>

      <p>
        At the time, the estimated number of maternal deaths in Kenya was around
        8,000, based on an estimated maternal mortality ratio of 510 per 100,000
        live births.
        The DHIS, meanwhile, recorded 945 maternal deaths in health facilities
        in 2014.
      </p>

      <p>
        The difference did not simply mean that one number was wrong.
        The two figures came from systems measuring maternal mortality in very
        different ways.
        The national estimate was attempting to capture maternal deaths across
        the whole population, including deaths that never touched a health
        facility at all.
        Two systems, asked what sounds like the same question, were actually
        measuring different things <mdash/> and the eight-fold gap between 945
        and 8,000 is what that difference looks like once it reaches a
        spreadsheet.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-five-systems">
      <title>Five systems, five different questions</title>

      <p>
        Kenya's data landscape for maternal mortality was not two sources but at
        least five, and each one was built to observe something distinct.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-mmr-sources">
        <title>
          What different sources and estimates tell us about maternal mortality
        </title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Source</cell>
            <cell>What is being observed</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>DHIS</cell>
            <cell>Maternal deaths reported through health facilities</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>MDSR</cell>
            <cell>
              Maternal deaths notified and investigated through the surveillance
              system
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Civil registration</cell>
            <cell>Registered deaths and their classified causes</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>DHS (household survey)</cell>
            <cell>Deaths reported retrospectively by surviving sisters</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>National / UN estimate</cell>
            <cell>
              A statistically modelled estimate based on multiple data sources
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        These are not five copies of one dataset with different error rates.
        The household survey approach, for instance, asks a respondent whether
        her sister died during pregnancy, delivery, or the postpartum period
        <mdash/> an estimate that depends entirely on what that respondent
        remembers and is willing to report, years later, about someone else's
        death.
        A facility system depends on a death being recorded at all, by a
        facility, while it was happening.
        Civil registration depends on a death certificate being completed and
        correctly classified.
        Each method has a different relationship to the truth it is trying to
        capture, before any question of data quality even arises.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-same-name">
      <title>Same column name, different definition</title>

      <p>
        Imagine a statistician receives three datasets to combine: one from
        DHIS, one from civil registration, one from a household survey.
        Each has a column called <c>maternal_death</c>, and in each, some rows
        are marked <c>TRUE</c>.
        The datasets look wonderfully compatible.
        Join them, and the merge runs without a single error.
      </p>

      <p>
        But what does <c>TRUE</c> actually mean in each dataset?
        In one, it means a facility recorded and reported the death.
        In another, it means a death certificate was completed and classified as
        maternal.
        In the third, it means a survey respondent recalled, possibly years
        later, that a sister had died around the time of a pregnancy.
        The join succeeds mechanically.
        It does not make the three <c>TRUE</c> s the same claim.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-mmr-variable-name">
        <title>A variable name is not a definition</title>

        <p>
          Two datasets can both contain a column called <c>maternal_death</c>,
          and the column can be produced by completely different processes, with
          different coverage, different timing, and different failure modes.
          Nothing about a successful join checks this.
          The software does not know, and cannot know, whether two columns with
          the same name mean the same thing; it only knows whether the rows line
          up.
          Before combining data from different sources, the question is not just
          <q>do the rows
          match?</q> It is
          <q>are the things being combined actually
          comparable?</q>
          <mdash/> and that question has to be answered by understanding where
          each dataset came from, not by inspecting the merged table afterward.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-kenya-findings">
      <title>What Kenya's own implementation found</title>

      <p>
        Smith et al.
        (2017), reporting directly on Kenya's MDSR implementation, describe
        exactly this gap turning into an operational problem.
        In 2014, only about 12% of the estimated annual number of maternal
        deaths were notified through the DHIS2 system at all.
        Of the 945 deaths that DHIS did record that year, only 484 (51.2%) were
        ever formally assessed.
        Of those 484 deaths, 447 (92.4%) were assessed as having received
        suboptimal care; in 394 cases (81.4%), the assessors judged that
        different management could have made a difference to the outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        The paper is explicit about why: under-reporting of maternal deaths;
        difficulty reviewing the data that was reported; suboptimal aggregation
        of cause-of-death information; and a persistent gap between DHIS and the
        Civil Registration and Vital Statistics system.
        Even after MDSR notification and review forms were integrated directly
        into DHIS, the paper reports,
        <q>the system has gaps, and
        the DHIS and Civil Registration and Vital Statistics system do not
        yet adequately capture all maternal deaths in Kenya.</q>
        While integrating the forms into DHIS improved the technical integration
        of the reporting process, it did not solve the underlying differences in
        coverage and definition between the systems, nor did it ensure that all
        maternal deaths were captured.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-not-unique-to-kenya">
      <title>Not unique to Kenya</title>

      <p>
        This is not a story about one country's data systems falling short.
        WHO's own guidance on maternal mortality measurement calls specifically
        for
        <q>robust collection of valid, reliable and
        standardized data,</q>
        for
        <q>improved quality and completeness of
        various data sources and systems, and linkages between them,</q>
        and for
        <q>appropriate integration, interpretation and use of
        data.</q>
        Guidance like this exists precisely because the gap between what
        different systems observe is a structural feature of maternal mortality
        measurement everywhere, not a defect specific to any one country's
        implementation.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 2023 systematic review and meta-analysis by Ahmed, Cresswell, and Say
        puts a number on how large that gap typically is.
        Across the studies included in their meta-analysis, maternal death
        recording was estimated to be 34% incomplete, with substantial variation
        between settings.
        After excluding two studies judged to have a high risk of bias, the
        authors estimated that maternal mortality was underestimated by 39%
        overall because of incompleteness and/or misclassification.
      </p>

      <p>
        Read this way, <q>data shapes</q> is not only about rows and columns.
        It is about what an observation and a variable actually represent
        <mdash/> and two datasets that look identical in structure can still be
        measuring entirely different things.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> World Health Organization (2022).
        <em>Maternal mortality measurement: guidance to improve national
        reporting.</em>
        Geneva: WHO.
        Smith, H., Ameh, C., Godia, P., Maua, J., Bartilol, K., Amoth, P.,
        Mathai, M.
        and van den Broek, N.
        (2017).
        <q>Implementing Maternal Death Surveillance and Response in
        Kenya: Incremental Progress and Lessons Learned.</q>
        <em>Global
        Health: Science and Practice</em>, 5(3), 345 <ndash/>
        354.
        Ahmed, S.M.A., Cresswell, J.A.
        and Say, L.
        (2023).
        <q>Incompleteness and
        misclassification of maternal death recording: a systematic review
        and meta-analysis.</q>
        <em>BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth</em>, 23, 794.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: JHU CSSE COVID-19 dashboard: Confirmed and verified.  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-jhu-shapes">
    <title>A Format That Grew a New Column Every Day</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In January 2020, a small team at Johns Hopkins University's Center for
        Systems Science and Engineering (CSSE) launched a public COVID-19
        dashboard, described the following May in a peer-reviewed paper by Dong,
        Du, and Gardner (2020).
        It became, within weeks, one of the single most-used sources of COVID-19
        data in the world <mdash/> the map that news outlets, governments, and
        researchers everywhere pulled numbers from.
        The underlying data was published openly, and it started out
        <term>wide</term>: one row per country or region, and one column for
        every date, holding that location's cumulative case count on that day.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-jhu-wide-problem">
      <title>A table with no final column</title>

      <p>
        Most wide tables have a fixed, known set of columns.
        This one did not.
        Every single day the pandemic continued, the dataset needed one more
        column than it had the day before, for as long as anyone was tracking
        the outbreak.
        Anyone who wanted to do more than eyeball the day's totals <mdash/> plot
        a trend, compute a weekly change, merge in a country's population to
        calculate a rate <mdash/> first had to reshape the table from wide to
        long, and had to do it again every time a new day's column was added.
        Wide format was not <em>hard</em> to use.
        It was hard to use
        <em>repeatedly, at
        growing scale, indefinitely.</em>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-covid-wide-format">
        <caption>
          Confirmed COVID-19 cases worldwide, 22 January to 2 February 2020.
          Each row represents a country or region, while each date is stored as
          a separate column.
          As more days are added, the dataset quickly becomes very wide.
          This is one of three datasets in the original collection: Confirmed
          Cases, Deaths, and Recovered.
        </caption>

        <description>
          A screenshot of a wide COVID-19 dataset.
          Rows represent countries and regions, with columns for location,
          latitude and longitude followed by daily confirmed case counts from 22
          January 2020 onwards.
          The many date columns illustrate how the dataset becomes increasingly
          wide as observations are recorded for each new day.
          This is the "Confirmed Cases" dataset; equivalent datasets were
          provided for deaths and recovered cases.
        </description>

        <image source="images/covid_wide_format.png" width="60%"/>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-jhu-switch">
      <title>Reshaping it themselves, and having the ground move</title>

      <p>
        JHU CSSE never turned this file long. Check the same repository today
        and the time-series files are exactly the shape they always were: one
        row per region, one column per date, still growing for as long as JHU
        kept updating it. Nobody at JHU fixed the wide-table problem, because
        for a dashboard glanced at once a day, wide was never actually broken.
        What changed was that the people who needed to do more than glance
        stopped waiting and reshaped it themselves.
      </p>

      <p>
        On 23 March 2020, the researcher Joachim Gassen published R code that
        pulled JHU's wide file and reshaped it to long <mdash/> one row per
        country per date <mdash/> specifically so it could be merged with
        other data. A wide table with a different date in every column has no
        single column two datasets can agree to join on; a long table does.
        Gassen's code went further, attaching standardised <term>ISO3c</term>
        country codes to every row, because without a shared identifier,
        nobody could reliably bring in a country's population to convert raw
        case counts into a per-capita rate.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-covid-long-format">
        <caption>
          The same confirmed-case numbers as <xref ref="fig-covid-wide-format"/>,
          reshaped to long format: one row per country per date, with a
          standardised country code attached for merging.
        </caption>

        <description>
          A long-format COVID-19 dataset with one row per country per date.
          Columns include a standardised ISO3c country code, country name,
          date, and confirmed case count, instead of one column per date.
        </description>

        <image source="images/covid_long_format.png" width="60%"/>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The reshaping held up for about a day. On 24 March 2020, Gassen
        published a follow-up post: <q>Just hours after my old blog post
        about tidying Johns Hopkins CSSE Covid-19 data the team has changed
        their time-series table data structure.</q> JHU had restructured the
        wide file's own columns <mdash/> adding latitude and longitude, and
        reorganising how US states were broken out <mdash/> a genuine
        improvement, but one that broke every piece of code written against
        the old column layout, Gassen's included. He rewrote and republished
        the same day.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-jhu-shapes-lesson">
        <title>
          Reshaping isn't a one-time fix. It's a job that keeps coming back.
        </title>

        <p>
          JHU never made its published file long, and it didn't need to
          <mdash/> wide genuinely was the right shape for a dashboard scanned
          once a day. The people who needed it long, to merge, to compute
          rates, to plot trends across countries, had to reshape it
          themselves. And because they did not control the source format, a
          change nobody warned them about broke that reshaping within a day
          of them publishing it. Wide and long are not a wrong version and a
          right version of the same table. They serve different readers, and
          whoever needs the other shape is the one who ends up doing the
          reshaping <mdash/> repeatedly, for as long as the source keeps
          changing underneath them.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Dong, E., Du, H.
        and Gardner, L.
        (2020).
        <q>An interactive web-based dashboard to track COVID-19 in real
        time.</q>
        <em>The Lancet Infectious Diseases</em>, 20(5), 533 <ndash/> 534.
        Gassen, J.
        (23 March 2020).
        <q>Tidying the John Hopkins Covid-19 data.</q>
        Blog post.
        Gassen, J.
        (24 March 2020).
        <q>Tidying the new Johns Hopkins Covid-19
        time-series datasets.</q>
        Blog post.
        Data repository:
        <url href="https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19" visual="github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: brief Ebola callback                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-ebola-callback">
    <title>A Shape Problem You've Already Met</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <xref ref="ss-ebola-transmission"/> described something that, looked at
        again, is a shape problem.
        Sierra Leone's Ebola response did not fail to merge its district data
        because anyone lost it or typed it wrong.
        It failed because
        <q>each district response centre
        had adopted its own method for recording cases,</q>
        and inconsistent Excel line-list formats across districts
        <q>caused ongoing problems
        merging and aggregating cases into national totals.</q>
        Different districts were not disagreeing about what happened.
        They were recording the same kind of information in incompatible shapes,
        the same failure mode as the JHU dataset above, just without a single
        format anyone had agreed to reshape toward.
      </p>

      <p>
        The two cases make the same point from opposite directions.
        JHU CSSE controlled one format and changed it once, deliberately, and
        every user benefited immediately.
        Sierra Leone's districts each controlled their own format independently,
        with no shared standard to converge on, and the cost was paid every time
        anyone tried to combine them.
        Agreeing on a shape in advance is far cheaper than reconciling
        incompatible shapes after the fact.
      </p>
    </introduction>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-applied">
    <title>Wide and Long, Applied</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> has always been in <term>long</term> format:
        one row per bird, one column per variable.
        That shape is why every <c>ggplot2</c> call in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> worked without any
        reshaping.
        But long format is not always what you want to <em>look</em> at, and
        reshaping between the two is a skill in its own right.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-long-to-wide">
      <title>From long to wide: making a report table</title>

      <p>
        Suppose you want a simple summary table, birds per lake per species,
        laid out the way a person reading a report would expect: lakes down the
        side, species across the top.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)

        counts_long &lt;- flamingos %&gt;%
          count(lake, species)

        head(counts_long)

        #&gt;          lake          species  n
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria Greater Flamingo 10
        #&gt; 2     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo 114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo 30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita  Lesser Flamingo 38
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru Greater Flamingo 26
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo 126
      </program>
      <p>
        <c>counts_long</c> is exactly the shape a computer wants: one row per
        lake-species combination, one column holding the count.
        It is not the shape a human wants to read at a glance.
        <c>pivot_wider()</c> turns the <c>species</c> values into column names.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        counts_wide &lt;- counts_long %&gt;%
          pivot_wider(names_from = species, values_from = n)

        counts_wide

        #&gt;          lake Greater Flamingo Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria               10             114
        #&gt; 2 Elementaita               30              38
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru               26             126
      </program>
      <p>
        Same numbers, same information, a different shape <mdash/> and this is
        <xref ref="table-lake-species-crosstab"/> from
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, arrived at mechanically rather than
        typed by hand.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-wide-to-long">
      <title>From wide to long: getting back to something plottable</title>

      <p>
        Reverse the process with <c>pivot_longer()</c>, and <c>counts_wide</c>
        becomes plottable again.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        counts_wide %&gt;%
          pivot_longer(cols = -lake, names_to = "species", values_to = "n")

        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 3
        #&gt;   lake        species              n
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Bogoria     Greater Flamingo    10
        #&gt; 2 Bogoria     Lesser Flamingo    114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo    30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Lesser Flamingo     38
        #&gt; 5 Nakuru      Greater Flamingo    26
        #&gt; 6 Nakuru      Lesser Flamingo    126
      </program>
      <p>
        This round trip, long <mdash/> wide <mdash/> long, is not busywork.
        Each direction serves a different reader.
        Long format serves R: <c>ggplot2</c>, <c>group_by()</c>, and joins all
        expect it.
        Wide format serves a person scanning a table by eye, or a spreadsheet
        cell reference that assumes species live in fixed columns.
        Knowing which one a task actually needs, before writing any code, saves
        exactly the repeated reshaping that cost Gassen a day's work when JHU's
        columns shifted under him, and exactly the merging failure that cost
        Sierra Leone's Ebola response.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  OBJECTIVES                                                       -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <objectives xml:id="objectives-data-shapes">
    <title>By the end of this topic you will be able to</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Distinguish wide and long data shapes, and identify which one a given
          dataset is in.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Explain why the same data, differently shaped, can make an operation
          trivial or nearly impossible.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Reshape data between wide and long using <c>pivot_wider()</c> and
          <c>pivot_longer()</c>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Recognise a merging or aggregation failure as a shape problem, not
          just a data-quality problem.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-data-shapes">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-jhu-daily">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-jhu-wide-problem"/> says a new column had to be added to
          the wide-format dataset every single day.
          Sketch, in words, what would have gone wrong for a downstream user's
          code if it referenced date columns by a fixed position (for example,
          <q>column 47</q> ) rather than by name.
          Would switching to long format on its own have fixed that particular
          problem?
          Why or why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-ebola-standard">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-shapes-ebola-callback"/> argues that agreeing on a
          shape in advance is cheaper than reconciling incompatible shapes
          afterward.
          Propose one concrete rule Sierra Leone's district response centres
          could have agreed to in advance <mdash/> about column names, row
          structure, or file format <mdash/> that would have prevented the
          merging problem described in <xref ref="ss-ebola-transmission"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-flamingo-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="ss-shapes-long-to-wide"/> as a model, build a wide
          table of mean <c>body_mass_g</c> by <c>lake</c> (rows) and
          <c>species</c> (columns), instead of counts.
          Which version, this one or <xref ref="table-lake-species-crosstab"/>,
          would you send to someone who wanted to read it in an email, and which
          would you hand to someone who wanted to plot it in <c>ggplot2</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-gapminder-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>gapminder</c> package from
          <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> is in long format:
          one row per country per year.
          Using <c>pivot_wider()</c>, build a table with one row per country and
          one column per year, holding <c>lifeExp</c>.
          Try plotting directly from this wide version with the same
          <c>ggplot2</c> code from <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d"/>.
          What breaks, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>